  heading: "Ranthambhore",

  html: "<p>Ranthambore National Park is located in district of southeastern Rajasthan in state of Rajasthan North India. The close town and railway station is at Sawai Madhopur; about 11 km left. This is one of largest and most poplar National Park in the country. Ranthambhor is around 180 kms from Jaipur by road; formerly this park was hunting land of Royal family and kings of Jaipur.</p>\n<p>This National Park, in 19 55 this was recognized as the Sawai Madhopur Game Sanctuary by the government of India and in 1973 the park was affirmed as one of the Indian project tiger reserves Ranthambore entitled as a national park in 1980. The park is sprawled of 392 sq kms located between Vindhyanchal and Aravali mountain range. The terrain comprises of open bush land, dense tropical dry forest, and rocky terrain inters extend with streams, ponds and lakes. The park is load at the edge of a highland, and is closely to Banas River in the north and Chambal River in Southern side. There are several lakes are exiting in the park, and named for the notable Ranthambhore bastion, located inside the park. Today Ranthambhore National Park is most sought after wildlife destinations and finest tiger reserve in the country. The park is paradise for widlife and photographers from all over the world. </p>\n<p>During your Jaipur tours you will have opportunity to observe rich culture and history of Jaisalmer, Rajasthan, India. Very interesting legends and history of Jaisalmer attract the tourists from far and far. The desert city of Jaisalmer was founded in the year of 1156 on the order of Rawal Jaisal, a Bhatti Rajput Chief after moving his capital here from his former capital Lodhurva. Legend of Jaisalmer says that a Brahmin hermit told Rawal Jaisal that Arjuna, one of five Pandava brothers, had came here and they forecasted that a ruler would one day build a magnificent fort along the crest known as Trikuta or Triple Peaked Hill. Trikuta Hill is that hill where the magnificent Golden Fort of Jaisalmer proudly stands. </p>\n<p>Ranthambhor National Park is famous for home of diurnal tigers; the park is for big tiger inhabitants, also the abode for various other species of mammals, reptiles and birds. Due to tribal and nearby villages, resulting increasing amounts of fatal human-tiger and poaching and acquaintances. The tiger project had been initiated in 1972 by Indian government, now the populations of tiger had been protected and now nos. of tigers is increasing. The park is one of the wonder places to witness these grand predators in the forest. According to present Indian Forest department assessment there are 40 tigers are existing in the Park, 14 males, 16 females and 10 cubs.. This was largely documented to sustained committed forest officials to control villagers poaching in the park. The hidden cameras were also fixed from corner to corner the reserve. These honest and hard efforts have been winning with motivation, now the park is having ample tigers. Here, the tigers can be even dotted during the day time</p>\n<p>The Park is having huge variety and numbers of animals, the namely: Tigers, Leopards, Sloth bears, Jackals, Jungle cats, Black bucks, Sambar deer, Chital, Striped Hyenas, Nilgai, Rufous tailed Hare, Coomon Yellow Bats, Macaques, Caracals, , Indian Wild Boar, Chinkara, Common mongoose, Small Indian Mongoose Common, Longeared Hedgehogs, Palm Civets or Toddy cat, Desert Cats, Five striped Palm Squirels, Indian False Vampires, Indian Flying Foxes, Indian Foxes, Indian Gerbilles, Indian Mole Rats, Indian Porcupines, Ratels, and Small Indian Civets many more.</p>\n<p>The park, due to diverse terrain and plenty of water bodies, has a sizable population of birds, migrants and resident. In total, lists of 272 species have been documented. Some of the best locations to watch birds at, and from, are Malik Talao, the Ranthambore Fort, Rajbagh Talao, Padam Talao and in the Jhalra area. The most important birds in Ranthambore includes Goose, Woodpeckers, Dove, Crakes, , Falcons, Pelicans, Cuckoo-Shrikes, Mynas Terns, Bulbul, Indian Gray Hornbills, Graylag, Common Kingfishers, Wagtails, Bee Eaters, Orioles, Cuckoos, Parakeets, Asian Palm Swift, Owl, Nightjars, Pigeon, Snipes, Sandpipers, Gulls, Great Crested Grebe, Egrets, Herons, Darters, Cormorants, , Bitterns, Wood Shrikes, Flamingos, Ibis , Storks, Pittas, Shrikes, Treepies, Crows, Minivets, Drongos, Flycatchers, Ioras, Pipits, Bayas, Sparrows, Finches, , Munias, etc.</p>\n<p>This park is the land of Reptiles: Snub Nosed Marsh Crocodiles, Shelled Turtles, North Indian Flap Shelled Turtles, Indian Cobras, Rat Snakes, Russel's, Banded Kraits, Indian Pythons, Vipers, Common Kraits Desert, Ganga Soft Tortoise, Indian Chamaeleon, Monitor Lizards and Saw-scaled Vipers, many more. </p>\n<p>The park is enriched with a variety of flora which mainly belongs to the dry deciduous type.nearly 300 species. The majority of the tree cover is however mainly vegetation are: Magnifera Indica, Tamarindicus indica, Accasia nilotica, Phoenix sylvestris, Syzygium cumini,Accacia catechu, Ficus benghalensis, Zizyphus mauritania, Butea monosperma(in plenty, Anogeossis Pendula, Authocephalus cadamba, Prosopis speciger, Capparis decidua, Mohua Madhuca indica, Flacourtia indica, Azadirachta indica.</p>\n<p>Due to the cruel summers and broad climatic conditions existing in the region, the flora and fauna of Ranthambore National Park have adapted themselves accordingly.</p>\n<p>Note: The Park will remain closed from 01 July till 30 Sep, every year due to heavy Monsoon.</p>\n<h4>Park Safari Timings: </h4>\n<p>1. October to March: Morning: 6.00 am to 9.00 am, Evening: 3.00 pm - 6.00 pm</p>\n<p>2 April to June: Morning: 6.30 am to 9.30 am, Evening: 3.30 pm - 6.00 pm</p>\n<p><strong>Best Time to visit:</strong> October - March and April to June.</p>\n<h4>Reach:</h4>\n<p><strong>By Air: </strong>Jaipur is nearest Airport 180 kms, 4 hours drive. </p>\n<p><strong>By Rail:</strong> Swai Madhopur Railway Station 11 kms from the Park area well connected with all the major cities. </p>\n<p><strong>By Road:</strong> Jaipur 180 kms, Ajmer Pushkar: 260 kms, Agra: 280 kms, Delhi 460 kms, Udaipur 405 kms, Jodhpur: 475 kms. </p>\n<h4>How to plan your tours:</h4>\n<p>Inbound travel agency and tour operators of Rajasthan and North India provide widlife tour packages for family, individuals to the inbound and off shore tourists, with all travel related services right from pick up from the international airport till the departure to your home town, tour planning, hotel bookings, assistance of queries about the tour, car and coach: surface transportation services, english and multilingual guide services, intercity travel, sightseeing, city tours, train and air ticketing etc., we are specialized to design the tour according to preferences of the guest, so if you want us to work out a exclusive tour for you let us know your preferred destinations, nos. of days for travel, travel period, preference of hotel category. Once we are updated with this feedback we will revert with the most adequate and ideal tour plan according to your interest with best of our suggestions and advise for betterment of your tour. To know more options of Indian tour plans, please visit: </p>\n<p>http://www.india- tourism.net/itineraries.htm to justify our credibility and reputation, kindly visit:http://www.india- tourism.net/testimonials.htm</p>\n<div"
